# What GitHub counts as this repository's languages.
#
# Linguist works by file extension, so `bench/` — the same loop written in six languages,
# so that the README's numbers can be re-derived by anyone rather than believed — would
# otherwise report this as part C, part Java, part Go and part Lua. Those files are
# fixtures for a measurement, not work. Marked vendored: still in the repository, out of
# the summary.
bench/**     linguist-vendored

# `editors/` is not marked. The VS Code extension is JavaScript, it was written here, and
# it does the job the compiler cannot do from inside a terminal — so it counts.

# `.lr` is Luarust's own extension, and Linguist has never heard of it, so the files
# actually written *in the language this repository is for* count as nothing at all. That
# stays true until Linguist learns the name, which wants the language used somewhere other
# than here. Naming them as Lua or Rust would make the bar look right by saying something
# false about what they are, so they go on counting as nothing.
*.lr         text
